"""
rainfall_yield_analysis.py
==========================
Tests whether rainfall has meaningful incremental impact on French winter
wheat yields beyond temperature, and compares the two drivers head-to-head.

Two-part output:

  Part 1 — Spearman ρ² comparison (same methodology as hedging_effectiveness_xgb.py)
    For each region, find the best rainfall index window (max |ρ| with yield)
    and the best temperature index window. Compare ρ² directly — this tells
    you how much yield variance each driver explains at its best.

  Part 2 — OLS incremental R² (does rainfall add power beyond temperature?)
    Stage 1: regress detrended yield on best temperature index → R²_temp
    Stage 2: add best rainfall index → R²_temp+rain
    Incremental R²: ΔR² = R²_temp+rain − R²_temp
    F-test for significance of ΔR² (H0: rainfall adds nothing).

Rainfall indices (analogous to temperature indices):
  RAI  = Σ precipitation              (cumulative, analogous to CAT)
  DRY  = Σ max(P_REF − P_d, 0)       (rainfall deficit, analogous to HDD)
  WET  = Σ max(P_d − P_REF, 0)       (excess rainfall, analogous to CDD)

  P_REF is set to the regional long-run daily mean, so DRY and WET are
  symmetric around the climatological baseline.

Methodology:
  Yield detrending:  MM-estimator + Wald test (Pelka §2, Finger 2010)
  Index selection:   max |Spearman ρ| (Möllmann et al. 2019 §2a)
  Incremental R²:    OLS F-test (Greene 2012, §5.4)
  Calibration only:  2000–2014 (prevents OOS data leakage into selection)
"""

import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
import warnings
import os
from scipy import stats
from scipy.optimize import minimize

